+-- DEFAULT-APPROVE-2 (2026-10-19, charter pillar autonomy; doctrine kernel IC-YES-001, AUTONOMY-FIRST-1). Follow-up to DEFAULT-APPROVE-1
+-- (migrations/2026-10-19-default-approve.sql, PR 2826, applied 11:17:54Z) that fixes the post-merge re-review findings F1 to F12 and the findings of the
+-- second re-review (R1 to R7, below).
+-- Owner rule 2026-10-19, verbatim: "ALL DECISIONS SHALL ALWAYS BE DEFAULT APPROVE ... Reversible fleet decisions SHALL ALWAYS take the
+-- recommended option and proceed without waiting". It does not override the interrupt gate (IC-GATE-001: irreversible AND identity-bound), the
+-- security and privacy limits (doctrine section 9), the harness permission checks, the shared-secrets lock or a live claim. A hard card the
+-- fleet approves is a blocker, and so is a net so wide that plainly reversible decisions are closed on a status-quo default again (card 115).
+-- APPLY-BY: ci
+-- DB: qnfo-audit
+-- BACKUP: bak_20261019_default_approve_2_triggers
+-- Rollback: UPDATE ops_config SET value = 'off', updated_at = datetime('now') WHERE key = 'default_approve_gate'; DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS default_approve_markers_moved_ins; DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS default_approve_markers_v2_guard_ins; DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS default_approve_markers_v2_guard_upd; DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS default_approve_markers_v2_guard_del; DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS default_approve_markers_v2_guard_loosen; UPDATE remediation_contracts SET verify_probe = REPLACE(verify_probe, 'v_human_action_decision_v2 d', 'v_human_action_decision d') WHERE class = 'fm-card-default-denies-decision'; DELETE FROM remediation_contracts WHERE class IN ('fm-default-approve-marker-health', 'fm-default-approve-hard-card-approved', 'belief-hard-card-not-approved'); DELETE FROM failure_modes WHERE fm_key IN ('default-approve-marker-health', 'default-approve-hard-card-approved'); DELETE FROM belief_registry WHERE belief_key = 'hard-card-not-approved'; DELETE FROM control_registry WHERE control = 'default-approve-hard-gate';
+-- Rollback scope: the first statement is the whole behavioural rollback of the approve path. The three triggers stay pointed at v_human_action_decision_v2 on
+-- purpose (dropping the view would make every human_actions insert fail); with default_approve_gate 'off' that view grades nothing approved and nothing
+-- held, so a decision card is closed on its stated default (DEFAULT-ADOPTED-1) or rerouted (AUTONOMY-FIRST-1). One difference from before DEFAULT-APPROVE-1
+-- stays on purpose: a card the gate reads fleet is handled by AUTONOMY-FIRST-1 only (R6), not closed on its default and queued as well.
+-- The other statements remove the guards and the registry rows; the marker table, both views, the widened v_human_action_gate and the first file's objects stay
+-- (the widened gate can only move a card from fleet to owner). To restore the DEFAULT-APPROVE-1 trigger texts and the previous gate view byte for byte, DROP the
+-- three triggers and the view and run the sql column of bak_20261019_default_approve_2_triggers.
+-- spof-review: none added. The triggers write autonomy_reroute_queue and decision_log (the tables the existing triggers write) and never
+-- agent_issues, so the AGENT-ISSUES-TRIGGER-DEPTH-1 chain and the QUEUE-BREAK-CYCLE-1 cycle break are unchanged.
+--
+-- WHAT THIS FILE DOES, by finding (decisions in brackets)
+-- R1 (blocker) The hard net was a lexical deny-list tuned to the cards of the first re-review; 81 further cards that are hard by meaning (money, signing, deleting,
+--    the owner's mailbox, name and accounts, credentials, privacy, suppression) in natural wording were approved at a high rate (67 of 81 with the gate forced to
+--    fleet). [Decision: the polarity of the last line of defence changes. A decision is approved only on a positive proof that it is about a fleet object: a
+--    fleet_proof marker (threshold, cadence, cap, window, flag, prompt, model, cache, index, migration, binding, panel, branch ... 137 stems) in title, why or action.
+--    The gate view's ELSE 'fleet' is no longer enough. A decision with no proof keeps the DEFAULT-ADOPTED-1 / AUTONOMY-FIRST-1 path, as it did before
+--    DEFAULT-APPROVE-1; the IDENTITY-BOUND: line still holds a card (card 115 carries both). worker.js and a pull request are where code lives, not what a card
+--    tunes, so a pull request is no proof; worker.js is only one of the stems and a hard marker still beats a proof.] The hard markers stay as a second net
+--    and grow: a verb net for outward effects (reply to, forward the, invite, share the folder, send a DM, sign up for, enter into, commit to, settle,
+--    reimburse, bill), the possessive owner s <object> (name, address, mail, account, byline ... 37 objects; the bare possessive is not a marker: 15 of the 111 live cards
+--    hold "owner's", plain fleet decisions among them), ISO currency codes in spaced and glued form, recurring
+--    amounts, crypto, and the credential, deletion, privacy and suppression phrases of the re-review. The 81 cards are the suite fixture
+--    (migrations/default-approve-2.cards.mjs): 0 of 81 x 8 shapes approve, 81 of 81 hard, with the gate forced to fleet by a pull request, by worker.js, with
+--    the act only in the title, the why or the action.
+-- R2 (major) Over-blocking: single-word noun stems made plainly reversible wording hard (tax, fee, paid, charge, fund, permanently, credential, password,
+--    oauth, inbox, mailbox, outlook, user data, from the list, sign the, consent, spend the, api key, e sign). [They are replaced by verb-object phrases tied
+--    to an external or irreversible effect: pay the fee, sales tax, is paid, charge the card, fund the, permanently delete, create or share a credential
+--    (a grid of 32 act verbs x credential, password and api key), sign the contract, ignore consent ... The destroying grid is split: bucket and backup,
+--    volume and corpus stay hard; database, namespace, data, table, rows, records, entries, objects, storage, ledger, snapshots, papers, audit logs and D1 are
+--    kind hard_destroy and are lifted by an exempt object (cache, tmp, temp, scratch, retry, dedupe, in memory, idx, branch), because resetting a retry table
+--    or clearing a cache namespace is reversible; an index is no longer a destroying noun.] The 30 probe cards of the re-review (rev2) and 40 more written for
+--    this repair (rev3) are suite fixtures and approve with the gate on fleet.
+-- R3 and R7 (major) The fold was a deny-list of 54 characters. [It now fails closed: after the fold any character outside printable ASCII, and any run of
+--    more than 64 spaces, makes the card hard, so every other separator, invisible or look-alike character (U+2002 to U+200A, U+2028, U+0085, U+200C, U+200D,
+--    U+2060, U+FEFF, U+FF0D, U+FE63, U+2043, U+201A, U+2032, U+00AB, fullwidth and Cyrillic look-alikes, emoji) is hard. The zero-width space and the soft hyphen
+--    are no longer folded to a space (they split a word, "pa<ZWSP>y"); they are residue. The fold keeps the 58 mapped separators, including the
+--    ellipsis, the arrows, the bullet, the middle dot and the multiplication sign, so ordinary typography in a card does not make it hard (2 of the 111 live
+--    cards hold one). The claim in CLAUDE.md and the policy is reworded to name what is folded and what fails closed.] The suite loops over every code
+--    point from U+0080 to U+3FFF that is not a letter or digit between "wire" and "transfer", and over 70 spaces.
+-- R4 (major) The IDENTITY-BOUND: contract was an exact ASCII string. [ib is also read on the folded text of title, why, action and the stated default: the label in
+--    any spelling that folds to "identity bound" (hyphen, underscore, en dash, non-breaking hyphen, a space before the colon, a full-width colon, no colon, **bold**,
+--    a zero-width space inside: the invisible characters are removed for this one test) or "owner only" holds the card. A card with such a character in its title,
+--    why or action is also hard (residue), so it is not approved either way. v_human_action_gate gets the same change in this file: its IDENTITY-BOUND: branch also reads IDENTITY_BOUND, IDENTITY__BOUND, IDENTITY___BOUND
+--    (LIKE's underscore is one character) and OWNER-ONLY:, with the live branch order (closure notice first). Without it a held spelling would still grade
+--    fleet and AUTONOMY-FIRST-1 would queue it. The suite pins 15 spellings.]
+-- R5 (major) Money shapes covered three currencies. [Spaced ISO 4217 codes (29 beyond usd, eur and gbp) and a glued digits-code and code-digits pair for each of
+--    the 32, recurring amounts (a month, per year, /mo), a budget, cost or fee with a digit, 10% fee, slang (bucks, quid, pounds, cents), crypto, credit
+--    limit, spend limit. Currency symbols other than the dollar sign are non-ASCII and fail closed through R3. This only narrows the leak; R1 bounds it.]
+-- R6 (major) A hard card on a fleet gate was closed on its default by human_actions_default_adopt_ai and also queued by AUTONOMY-FIRST-1 ("Decide and
+--    execute"), a card both resolved and queued. [Decision: keep the hard card on the old path, and make that path coherent. The hard marker selects the
+--    wording, it is not a second interrupt gate (a hard condition on the autonomy trigger was measured on the 111 live cards and would put plain fleet decisions
+--    such as 20, 26 and 30 back on the owner); the barrier for an owner-reserved act is the gate view's owner words and the IDENTITY-BOUND: line. The
+--    contradiction is removed at its cause: a card the gate reads fleet (and that is not a closure notice) is now handled by exactly one trigger, AUTONOMY-FIRST-1
+--    or the approve trigger, so it ends rerouted with one queue row; the adopt trigger keeps owner-gate cards. The queue text of a hard card tells the fleet to
+--    do only the reversible part and to file any step the interrupt gate keeps as an IDENTITY-BOUND: card. CLAUDE.md, the policy and the control record say
+--    so.]
+-- F3 (urgent) The markers are LIKE and GLOB patterns read from a table by three trigger WHENs on every human_actions insert, and D1 rejects a
+--    pattern over 50 bytes at run time, so one long row INSERTed as CLAUDE.md invited would have failed every insert and every owner-request close.
+--    [The old table cannot take a fifth kind (its CHECK names four) and cannot be rebuilt without a window in which the old view fails, so the markers
+--    move to a NEW table default_approve_markers_v2; the old table is left in place and now refuses an INSERT with a pointer.] The new table has
+--    a CHECK (3 to 50 bytes, not made only of wildcards) and BEFORE INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE triggers with the same rule, and a hard, hard_glob or hard_destroy
+--    row is changed or removed only while ops_config default_approve_marker_edit holds a decision id, which a migration sets and clears (this file needs no
+--    edit: it only inserts). A row that widens what the fleet approves (destroy_exempt, fleet_proof) is added only under the same key (R1, R2).
+--    Detector fm-default-approve-marker-health reads: no pattern over 50 or under 3 bytes, at least 1671 hard, hard_glob and hard_destroy rows (the number
+--    shipped here; a migration that removes one lowers it), and that key empty.
+-- F1 (blocker, first re-review) The fold. The view lower-cases title, why and action, maps 58 characters (ASCII punctuation, tab, CR, LF, VT,
+--    FF, no-break, thin and narrow no-break spaces, the hyphen and dash variants U+2010 to U+2015, the curly quotes, the minus sign, the ideographic space, the
+--    ellipsis, two arrows, the bullet, the middle dot, the multiplication sign) to a space and collapses runs of up to 64 spaces, so a marker is written once
+--    in the spaced form: one chain of 58 nested replace() calls plus 6 collapse steps, depth 64 against D1's 100 [layering it in subqueries was tried and lost
+--    the rowid push-down, ~0.4 ms against ~2.4 ms a lookup on 120 rows in a scratch test, so it is one GROUP BY id subquery; D1 accepted a 90-deep nested call
+--    as a SELECT on 2026-10-19]. The stated default is folded in the same pass, ahead of a boundary word, and cut off again, so ib can read it and hard cannot.
+--    The shapes are kind hard_glob, matched by GLOB on the lower-cased RAW text: a dollar amount, an e-mail address as sixteen patterns, one per domain-label
+--    length (the re-review's single pattern spans spaces and made "wrangler@4.1.0 ... package.json" hard), recurring and glued amounts. hard is judged on
+--    title, why and action only: the stated default is the status quo and often negates the act ("No payment is made"). [consent is no longer a bare word.]
+-- Decision detection (F8): the action markers option 1, select one, yes: no:, accept: reject:, 1) 2); the title marker approve %; the title
+--    exclusions has, have, will, who, when, where, was, were; a decision_action hit overrides a title exclusion ("Is X ok? Pick one").
+-- Views: v_human_action_decision_v2 has the columns of the old view plus proof, and is created with IF NOT EXISTS; the old view is left in place and its
+--    only remaining reader, fm-card-default-denies-decision, is re-pointed. v_human_action_decision_v2_probe is the same text over four sentinel
+--    rows (for the belief probe; the union is kept out of the main view because it defeats the push-down). approve and held also require
+--    ops_config autonomy_first_gate <> off (F6: one operator switch stops both paths, as CLAUDE.md says for the reroute). approve now needs proof = 1 (R1).
+-- Triggers (F12): human_actions_default_approve_ai is rebuilt first, then human_actions_default_adopt_ai and human_actions_autonomy_first_ai, each DROP
+--    adjacent to its CREATE. A decision card inserted between rebuilds is handled by at least one trigger (the unrebuilt ones read the old view,
+--    which agrees with the new one on the cards both call approve or held; a card they disagree on can be queued twice or stay open, never
+--    approved by a hard marker). The queue descriptions put "Card action:" and "Charter pillar: autonomy." on lines of their own, so a code-task:
+--    line in a card's action starts a line and the code loop can build it (F4); the category stays governance (planner trust is a separate change,
+--    CODE-LOOP-CONTROL-PLANE-1, PR 2850).
+-- F2 [decision, restated under R6: human_actions_autonomy_first_ai does NOT gain AND hard = 0]. Measured by replaying the view over all 111 live human_actions
+--    rows (SELECT only): the live gate-fleet cards that grade hard are 20 (cut back AI spend: a dollar amount), 23 (an owner-voice launch post), 26 (an outreach
+--    cap: gdpr and cold email), 28 (add Cloudflare secrets: a credential) and 30 (merge a pull request; an e-mail address). Stopping the reroute of 20, 26 and 30
+--    would put plain fleet decisions back on the owner.
+-- F5, F11 Records: a corrective decision_log row restates the ids from the live view (the first file's row names the wrong ones; INSERT only);
+--    control_registry default-approve-hard-gate (kind gate, disposition keep, alternate path = the IDENTITY-BOUND: line and the owner queue, held-card
+--    expiry decided: none automatic, named by v_blocker_claims / queue_sla blocker-unverified / unverified_blocker_claims after 60 minutes);
+--    belief_registry hard-card-not-approved with probe belief-hard-card-not-approved (four sentinel rows through the probe view must read
+--    decision 1, hard 1, approve 0); failure mode default-approve-hard-card-approved (every card DEFAULT-APPROVE-1 rerouted, graded with
+--    today's markers, must read hard 0).
+-- Not done here, with the reason: F13, the gate view's branch order differs between the repo file (2026-10-19-owner-notice-gate.sql) and the live view (closure
+--    notice first, graded 'receipt'); this file now installs the live order, so the repo and the live text agree after it (the notice-gate file is left as it is:
+--    re-applying it would drop this view again). F9 is the suite; F10 is CLAUDE.md and docs/AUTONOMY-DECISION-POLICY.md.
+-- Verified before this file was written (SELECT only): the three live trigger texts and the live view in sqlite_master equal the texts of the first
+--    file; the live gate view equals the text installed here apart from the widened label branch; the view replayed over all 111 live rows grades approve 19, 21, 41,
+--    56 (as before), held 115 (76 reads hard under the github account marker), decision cards that are hard on a fleet gate 20, 23 and 26; D1 refuses a LIKE
+--    pattern over 50 bytes with error 7500.
+-- Offline suite: migrations/default-approve-2.test.mjs replays the live text of the three triggers and the gate view, the DEFAULT-APPROVE-1 file,
+-- then this one.
+
+-- 0. backup of the three trigger texts and the gate view as they are before this file (the DEFAULT-APPROVE-1 texts, which read v_human_action_decision)
+CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS bak_20261019_default_approve_2_triggers AS
+  SELECT name, type, sql, strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ', 'now') AS saved_at
+    FROM sqlite_master
+   WHERE (type = 'trigger' AND name IN ('human_actions_default_approve_ai', 'human_actions_default_adopt_ai', 'human_actions_autonomy_first_ai'))
+      OR (type = 'view' AND name = 'v_human_action_gate');
+
+-- 1. the marker table (new, so its CHECK can name hard_glob), its guards (F3) and its rows
+CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS default_approve_markers_v2 (kind TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (kind IN ('hard', 'hard_glob', 'hard_destroy', 'destroy_exempt', 'fleet_proof', 'decision_action', 'decision_title', 'decision_title_not')), pattern TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (length(CAST(pattern AS BLOB)) BETWEEN 3 AND 50 AND TRIM(pattern, CASE WHEN kind = 'hard_glob' THEN '*?' ELSE '%_' END) <> ''), note TEXT, added_at TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ', 'now')), PRIMARY KEY (kind, pattern));
+CREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS default_approve_markers_v2_guard_ins BEFORE INSERT ON default_approve_markers_v2
+WHEN length(CAST(NEW.pattern AS BLOB)) > 50 OR length(CAST(NEW.pattern AS BLOB)) < 3 OR TRIM(NEW.pattern, CASE WHEN NEW.kind = 'hard_glob' THEN '*?' ELSE '%_' END) = ''
+BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ABORT, 'DEFAULT-APPROVE-2: a marker pattern is 3 to 50 bytes and not a bare wildcard (D1 rejects a LIKE or GLOB pattern over 50 bytes at run time, which would fail every human_actions insert)'); END;
+CREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS default_approve_markers_v2_guard_upd BEFORE UPDATE ON default_approve_markers_v2
+WHEN length(CAST(NEW.pattern AS BLOB)) > 50 OR length(CAST(NEW.pattern AS BLOB)) < 3 OR TRIM(NEW.pattern, CASE WHEN NEW.kind = 'hard_glob' THEN '*?' ELSE '%_' END) = ''
+  OR ((OLD.kind IN ('hard', 'hard_glob', 'hard_destroy') OR NEW.kind IN ('destroy_exempt', 'fleet_proof')) AND (NEW.kind <> OLD.kind OR NEW.pattern <> OLD.pattern) AND TRIM(COALESCE((SELECT value FROM ops_config WHERE key = 'default_approve_marker_edit'), '')) = '')
+BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ABORT, 'DEFAULT-APPROVE-2: a marker pattern is 3 to 50 bytes and not a bare wildcard, and a hard, exempt or proof marker is changed only by a migration that sets ops_config default_approve_marker_edit to its decision id and clears it'); END;
+CREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS default_approve_markers_v2_guard_del BEFORE DELETE ON default_approve_markers_v2
+WHEN OLD.kind IN ('hard', 'hard_glob', 'hard_destroy') AND TRIM(COALESCE((SELECT value FROM ops_config WHERE key = 'default_approve_marker_edit'), '')) = ''
+BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ABORT, 'DEFAULT-APPROVE-2: a hard marker is removed only by a migration that names the reason and sets ops_config default_approve_marker_edit to its decision id (and clears it after)'); END;
+CREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS default_approve_markers_moved_ins BEFORE INSERT ON default_approve_markers
+BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ABORT, 'DEFAULT-APPROVE-2: the markers moved to default_approve_markers_v2 (kinds hard, hard_glob, hard_destroy, destroy_exempt, fleet_proof, decision_action, decision_title, decision_title_not); this table feeds only the retired view v_human_action_decision'); END;
